Chapter 1023: The Search for the Best Possible Outcome


——The Lugunica Royal Castle, which he had looked up at countless times, was far away, its chalk-white structure seeming to be swallowed by the evening sky.

"——Emilia."

As he murmured that single word, Subaru felt a wrenching grief in his chest.

Emilia was imprisoned within that castle at the pinnacle of the royal capital. To one day seat her upon the throne of that place was Subaru's wish, and absolutely not to have her bound in a dungeon.

"...Emilia is a royal selection candidate. At the very least, she should be treated courteously on the surface. There's no way she'd be thrown into a dungeon."

"I know. I know that. But that's not the issue..."

"——"

"Ah, sorry... You were just trying to cheer me up."

"It's fine, I suppose. Being able to encourage Subaru when he's overwhelmed is the privilege of a partner, I suppose. I wonder if Petra and Rem would be frustrated with jealousy."

"Petra might be, but I'm not so sure about Rem. I don't think she'd be frustrated."

Letting out a bitter smile, Subaru was surprised at himself for being able to do so.

Just moments ago, thinking of his own powerlessness and Emilia whom he had no choice but to leave behind, his heart had felt like it was about to burst. Yet, Beatrice's presence was immense.

Glancing at her, she gave him an adorably smug look, and another small sigh escaped his lips.

—Subaru, Beatrice, and the members who had been at the "Six Tongues" safe house were, under Russel's direction, preparing to move as planned.

The safe house had been, quite boldly, one of the largest warehouses in the commercial district. Located near the defensive wall adjacent to the noble district, it left a strong impression, giving them a glimpse of the "Six Tongues" mastery of covert operations—how they blended into the royal capital and existed there so naturally.

Now, Subaru and the others had moved around to the back of the safe house, gazing at the Lugunica Royal Castle, where Emilia should be, until the very last moment before their escape preparations were complete.

"——"

The main members included Crusch, who remained unconscious and asleep; Lachins, who, unlike Subaru and the others, showed no resistance to escaping the capital; along with "White Sheep" and "Black Dog"—led by Russel. But it seemed there were also multiple other personnel in charge of disguise and information obfuscation.

As expected of an intelligence agency directly under the kingdom, the scale of everything they did was enormous.

"But even with all that power, we're in a position of having been thoroughly beaten, I suppose."

"Yeah, but we won't stay beaten. That's what this escape is for—a running start to fight back."

"A running start, I suppose. —That's a little unexpected, I suppose."

To Subaru, who voiced his undefeated resolve, Beatrice, holding his hand, murmured that. Hearing this, Subaru tilted his head. "Unexpected?"

Beatrice furrowed her brows slightly.

"That Subaru was able to decide to leave the royal capital so quickly, I suppose. Leaving Emilia behind, and not meeting up with Otto and the others, must have been painful choices."

"That's... definitely true, and it still is now. Before I went to Miklotov's place, I was talking with Emilia-tan about what to have for lunch. She said she was making a lunchbox with Rem and Petra, so I was looking forward to it... and now, this."

"Subaru..."

"It's painful, it's hard. But, the fact that you didn't oppose it either means you know this is for the best, right? You too."

To Subaru, whose voice had dropped pathetically, after a moment, Beatrice gave a small nod.

She hadn't spoken up at that time. Beatrice, too, had been certain that remaining in the royal capital would inevitably lead to a losing battle. Even so, the reason she hadn't rushed Subaru's decision was out of consideration for him, and for his own acceptance.

So that he wouldn't just give up reluctantly because someone told him to, but so he would choose for himself.

"You're cute and strict, my Beako."

"And this cuteness and strictness is guaranteed for life, I suppose."

"What a bargain. I'm glad I signed up, Beako Insurance."

Ruffling her head, Subaru felt his heart cleansed by his partner's reliability.

Normally, she would have quickly escaped, crying "Stop it, I suppose~!", but the fact she let him do it this time was likely also Beatrice's consideration for Subaru's feelings.

Just then—

"The preparations for departure are nearly complete. Are you both alright?"

The one who called out was "White Sheep".

Over her black suit, she wore a white robe—the same "Perception Obstruction" robe that Subaru and the others wore—and had let down her tied-up hair, subtly changing her atmosphere.

It was a style of disguise combining "Perception Obstruction" with a light change in appearance.

"Huh."

"W-What is it? Is there something strange about me?"

"Ah, sorry, sorry, that's not it. I just meant your impression changes a lot from when you're just in the suit. If anything, your current look gives off more of a 'White Sheep'-chan vibe."

"That might... be true. Before I was picked up by 'Silver Fox'... when I was with Mama, I wore clothes like this more often."

"Ah, sorry. Made you remember something unpleasant, didn't I."

Subaru apologized for making her recall a bad memory, but "White Sheep" just gave a faint smile and said, "No."

Then she stroked her own long, dark blue hair.

"...I'm the one who should be sorry."

"—? What for?"

"Even though it was for our convenience... we rushed you. Normally, Natsuki Subaru-san, you would have wanted more time to decide, too."

Lowering her eyes, "White Sheep" spoke as if she were the one pained, and Subaru couldn't help but give a bitter smile.

Having left Capella's underlings, the "Beloved Children", she was now a member of the "Six Tongues" led by Russel. But at her core, her good nature certainly made her unsuited to being a villain's subordinate or a spy's colleague. After all, she was even trying to shoulder the burden of the time Subaru took to make his choice.

"You don't have to take on my burdens too. I don't feel rushed. In fact, I'm grateful you gave me the information I needed to decide."

"Natsuki Subaru-san..."

"But if you still feel hesitant no matter what, then please stop that."

"Huh?"

"Calling me by my full name, 'Natsuki Subaru-san', that is. Natsuki-san or Subaru-san is fine, but I prefer one or the other. It makes me tense up."

Shrugging his shoulders, Subaru made the request, playing up a casual tone as much as possible. "White Sheep's" eyes widened, and for a moment, she opened and closed her mouth wordlessly.

At that "White Sheep," Beatrice, beside Subaru, let out a large sigh.

"There's no need to read too much into it, I suppose. He's just saying what he means, literally, I suppose."

"What's with that unnecessary disclaimer... What other meaning could there be?"

"I don't know, I suppose~. I'm not telling, I suppose~."

"That's exactly the way someone who does know talks!"

To Beatrice, who covered her ears and turned her face away, Subaru pouted petulantly. Then, as if the wind had been taken out of her sails by Subaru and Beatrice's exchange, the tension suddenly drained from the shoulders of "White Sheep," who had been dumbfounded.

And then—

"—Subaru-san and Beatrice-san. Is that alright?"

"Yeah, that's fine. You can even call me Betchin if you want."

"Betchin!? Even Subaru hasn't called me that, I suppose!?"

"I-It doesn't seem like I'd get a reply, so I'll stick with this..."

Reacting with extreme modesty, "White Sheep" relinquished the historic right to call Beatrice "Betchin". However, showing no lingering attachment to that, "White Sheep" instead looked at Subaru with eyes harboring a different kind of concern.

Just as Subaru frowned at her demeanor, her lips moved simultaneously—

"Um, Subaru-san, Ti—"

"—Oi! How long are you bastards gonna dawdle! Get a move on!"

But at that moment, Lachins barged in, his voice rough and grating.

Under the sharp glare of Lachins' four-white eyes, sharpened further, "White Sheep" cowered with an "Eep!", shrinking her shoulders. Shielding "White Sheep" behind him, Subaru stared at Lachins.

"You seriously do not look good in that robe."

"Shut up, mind your own business! And why the hell do I gotta be bossed around by Russel Fellow? Make the guys you signed slave contracts with do the work."

"W-We aren't slave contracts or anything!"

"'White Sheep'-chan, panicking like that makes it sound suspiciously true..."

Chiding "White Sheep" for taking Lachins' foul mouth so seriously, Subaru once again gazed at the distant castle and let out a long sigh. —Just praying for her safety.

"I wonder if Emilia is praying for the same thing for Subaru, I suppose."

"For us. That would make me happier than just for me, and it's more like Emilia."

Answering Beatrice, who could discern his inner thoughts with a single glance, Subaru shook off his lingering attachment, turned his back to the castle, and began to walk.

The plan was to pass through the safe house and board the dragon carriage parked out front.

Just then, inside the quickly emptying safe house, he spotted a robed back trying to carry out a box that was about an armful in size.

He didn't know what was inside the box, but seeing them struggle with its considerable weight—

"Should I carry this to the dragon carriage outside?"

"Ah..."

Quickly offering a hand, Subaru lifted the box in their stead with a heave—no, 'heave' was a lie. It was quite heavy, requiring a bit more like a 'hup' of power.

Fortunately, he managed to lift it alone, so he saved face. As he felt relieved inwardly, the person he'd been wrestling with the box for—a figure with a hood pulled low—reacted with bewilderment.

"—? Are you okay?"

To this bewildered person, Subaru tilted his head.

The hood hid their face, but they were slender. However, in terms of hidden identity, "Black Dog" was far more suspicious, so that wasn't a particular reason to suspect them.

Rather than suspicion, what made Subaru curious was the person's awkward reaction. But without time to question it, the figure bowed their head and scurried away quickly.

"Not even saying thanks, how rude, I suppose."

"Well, some people are just shy. Not everyone's as friendly and adorable as Garfiel."

"Why bring up Garfiel there... Well, I can't say I don't understand, I suppose."

Charm isn't just about looks; it shows in one's attitude and actions.

As Subaru and Beatrice nodded at each other, sharing this common view of their camp, "White Sheep," noticing Subaru holding the luggage, called out "Excuse me."

"Making Subaru-san carry that... I'll tell 'Blue Snake'-san about it later."

"No, no, this much is nothing... Wait, that person just now was 'Blue Snake'? That's the person who healed my burns, right?"

"Eh, ah, yes. They're a very skilled user of healing magic."

Reacting to the code name—"Blue Snake"—Subaru confirmed with "White Sheep," who affirmed it. With Sheep, Dog, and Fox, having a healer be Snake was quite stylish.

In Subaru's knowledge, snakes and medicine were inextricably linked. There was even a story that the staff of Asclepius, the doctor in Greek mythology, had a single snake wrapped around it.

Of course, the connection to the name "Blue Snake" was probably a coincidence.

"In that case, I should be the one thanking them instead. Carrying some luggage isn't nearly enough to repay that favor."

"Ah, but that might be difficult. 'Blue Snake'-san doesn't seem to like talking much with others... 'Silver Fox' also told us to respect that wish."

"Hmm? A misanthropic doctor. ...Sounds more and more like a brilliant physician."

Skilled but eccentric—that was like the prerequisite for famous doctors in manga and anime.

That said, accepting "White Sheep's" point, Subaru gave up on contacting "Blue Snake". Instead, he decided to pay back the favor bit by bit, helping if they struggled with carrying luggage again.

Anyway—

"—Let's go. So we can come back again."

So declaring, as if to convince none other than himself, Natsuki Subaru, accompanied by Beatrice who stayed close by, stepped out of the safe house.

△▼△▼△▼△

"—The principle of 'Perception Obstruction' is to slightly alter the impression, the aura, and the way they are emitted that an object originally possesses, minimizing the immediate sense of wrongness each time. In other words—"

"In other words?"

"—Under a strict security network, it is nothing more than a placebo. Please do not overestimate it."

Told this by Russel, who sat facing him inside the dragon carriage, Subaru gave a small nod while pulling his own and the adjacent Beatrice's hoods slightly deeper.

They were in a convoy of dragon carriages disguised as a merchant group leaving the royal capital, as arranged by the "Six Tongues".

The prepared carriages weren't high-class ones. They were standard-issue, unadorned types, like those used by peddlers on the highways, let alone in the royal capital. To disguise as a merchant group, the convoy wasn't just one or two, but on the scale of ten carriages, which made Subaru worry that this in itself might draw attention—

"Please rest assured. Currently, the royal capital is in a state of slight tension. Many are choosing to temporarily leave the capital to avoid this. We are also taking advantage of that."

"By 'state of tension,' you mean..."

"Of course, it's the fear that a dangerous fugitive may be hiding within the city. A significant number of Royal Guards have been mobilized, and a heavy investigation is underway. Lord McMahon's death is being kept from the public, but the air of an emergency cannot be hidden."

"Well, naturally... Everyone must be filled with anxiety."

Having his prediction of the capital's atmosphere affirmed, Subaru felt pity for the people of the capital caught up in "Lust's" machinations. Then, Russel narrowed his eyes slightly.

"Natsuki-dono, do you worry so much not only for Emilia-sama and your camp members, but also for the citizens of the royal capital?"

"Eh? Well, I wouldn't go so far as to say worry, but I do wonder if they're okay. Even Capella wouldn't... I can't say for sure that she wouldn't use her authority to pointlessly replace citizens, which might be the source of my feelings."

As a member with decision-making rights in the royal castle, replacing the "Sage Council" or high-ranking nobles using her authority was precisely the optimal move for a takeover of the nation. In comparison, using her authority on citizens would be nothing more than harassment, but Capella was the type who might gleefully do just that.

Of course, her actions in Priestella likely aimed to intentionally publicize her existence and the effects of the "Lust" authority, keeping the "Six Tongues" fully occupied—

"— —"

"...Hey, Russel-san?"

Suddenly, having thought that far, Subaru noticed Russel was silent.

At Subaru's call, Russel, who had been stroking his beard with his finger with one eye closed, said "No," briefly continuing his words.

"Natsuki-dono's concerns are quite valid. I don't know if it will ease your mind, but the 'Six Tongues' is not withdrawing entirely from the royal capital at once. A not-insignificant number will remain for intelligence gathering and disruption activities. Please place your trust in their efforts."

"Yes, I'm sorry, but we have no choice but to leave that to you, Russel-san. If only I had ten or twenty arms, and five or six bodies, I could be of more use..."

"What a preposterous hypothetical, I suppose. Subaru is perfectly fine just as Subaru is, I suppose."

"I'm grateful for that so-so evaluation. ...Anyway, you and your people are the only ones we can rely on on that side. Show us the true power of the intelligence agency 'Six Tongues'."

When Subaru showed a tightly clenched fist, Russel nodded in acknowledgment. Feeling reassured by his response, Subaru became conscious of the rear of the dragon carriage—another carriage behind them.

As mentioned, the merchant group convoy disguised by the "Six Tongues" numbered over a dozen carriages. The one Subaru and the others were riding in was at the head, with personnel riding separately. What Subaru was especially concerned about was Crusch, who hadn't woken up even once at the safe house.

Since falling asleep in the sewers, her consciousness had not returned.

Just before falling asleep, Crusch had mentioned the influence of the "Dragon Eye," but that was based on self-diagnosis. In reality, they knew nothing at all about what that "Dragon's Eye" was.

Beatrice had said there were no factors other than fatigue, but—

"Are you concerned about Crusch-sama's condition?"

"Well, yes. But it's the same for you, isn't it? If you want to borrow Crusch-san's power to fight the 'Beloved Children', aren't you anxious?"

"We are certainly worried. However, someone whose diagnostic ability is arguably second to none has stated there are no concerns regarding Crusch-sama's body itself. Of course, we do have concerns about this 'Dragon's Eye' you mentioned, Natsuki-dono..."

"So for now, all we can do is treat her with care and wait patiently for her to wake up."

"Precisely."

Having his concerns addressed politely, Subaru sighed at the multitude of things beyond his power.

Indeed, it was the "Six Tongues" who prioritized Crusch's safety above all else. There was no doubt they would do their utmost for her. Even assigning a skilled healer—

"Speaking of which, 'Blue Snake'-san, was it? I saw them briefly earlier. Are they looking after Crusch-san?"

"—. You met 'Blue Snake'?"

"Huh? Ah, it was just passing by right before we left. They didn't speak to me. 'White Sheep'-chan mentioned it too, they're a shy person, huh."

"...Indeed. That's largely correct. 'Blue Snake' is also attending to Crusch-sama. Please rest assured."

Perhaps it was because he was so surprised that the misanthropic "Blue Snake" had met Subaru, but finding Russel's reaction—taking a moment before answering for the first time—fresh, Subaru felt relieved hearing that "Blue Snake" was with Crusch. But on the other hand—

"Ferris must be really worried too."

Dismissed from his position as Crusch's number one knight, Ferris had rejected Subaru's offer to mediate between them. —His whereabouts after they parted at the capital's cemetery were unknown, but he was certainly still in the royal capital. Naturally, he would have heard about this disturbance by now.

At the very least, Subaru hoped he had met up with Wilhelm and wouldn't do anything rash— he thought just then.

"—Stopped?"

Suddenly sensing the dragon carriage stop, Subaru instinctively tensed up. Immediately, he put an arm around Beatrice's shoulder, trying to prepare for a sudden situation.

But at Subaru's reaction, Russel across from him smiled and said, "Please rest assured."

"It seems we've reached the waiting line for the inspection at the royal capital's main gate. It's usually crowded, but it looks like we'll be waiting quite a while."

"Waiting quite a while? Even though it's almost night, are that many people leaving the capital?"

"Only some—those with quick ears, those with quick judgment, those sharp about profit and loss—but for such people, day or night is irrelevant. Tomorrow, as this atmosphere spreads further, more people may start moving."

"It's just like a replay of the Empire, I suppose. Back then, many people fled the Imperial Capital too, I suppose."

"I was just thinking the same thing. I definitely don't want it to turn out like the Imperial Capital."

Nodding at Beatrice's words, Subaru recalled the Imperial Capital of Lupugana in Vollachia.

It was still fresh in his memory how, under Abel's instructions, there was a mass evacuation of Imperial citizens from the capital, which had been turned into a city of corpses by Sphinx leading the "Great Calamity". But that was possible only because Vollachia had a strong top-down system; demanding the same in Lugunica would be difficult.

Moreover, unlike the undead, there was no way to distinguish Capella's "Beloved Children".

"That is why Crusch-sama's divine protection exists, and that is why we exist. —Natsuki-dono, I'm sure this goes without saying for you, but please, always search for the best possible outcome."

Subaru looked back directly at Russel, whose tone had dropped slightly. Russel had spoken with the face not of a merchant, but of the head of an intelligence organization—the Chief of the "Six Tongues".

Those words, likely a self-admonishment that it was the most important thing of all.

"The best possible... outcome..."

"Yes. For the kingdom, for the future, for those dear to you, whatever it may be—to reach the ultimate destination, always pursue the best possible outcome. So that you can choose even the choices that involve pain, if that is what it takes to achieve it."

"— —"

It was a detached tone, devoid of passion. But that didn't mean it failed to strike Subaru's heart.

Certainly, words laden with passion and appealing to emotion can strike the heart. But the reason Russel's words felt devoid of passion was because they had already been struck and forged so many times they would no longer change shape.

You strike iron while it's hot to forge it into the desired shape. —However, there's no point in heating iron that has already taken its shape, striking it, and deliberately distorting it.

"—It seems we've arrived at the inspection. I will go deal with it, so please wait."

Called by the coachman through the small window of the dragon carriage, which had repeated stopping, advancing, and stopping again, Russel stood up and stepped down from the passenger car.

Watching his back, Subaru let out a large sigh. Then, Beatrice beside him said,

"Good grief, I suppose. That man... being friends with Roswaal, I'm convinced, I suppose."

"As expected of Beako. I was thinking the same thing. The way they lean their vectors is similar, just the direction they face is different. ...The search for the best possible outcome, huh."

"Subaru is always doing that, I suppose. Betty will vouch for that, I suppose."

Beatrice seemed indignant, as if Subaru had been accused of slacking off.

Of course, Russel likely didn't have such an intention, and Beatrice knew that too. At the same time, she likely also knew that capable people are always plagued by such frustration.

"—The inspection seems pretty heavy."

Standing up, Subaru opened the small window leading to the coachman's stand a little, peeking outside. Side-by-side with Beatrice, who peeked in saying "I suppose," they observed the usual checkpoint set up at the capital's main gate, and the separate temporary watchtowers and waiting areas built apart from it.

At the checkpoint, they seemed to be requesting travel permits, cargo manifests, and the like—things that wouldn't normally be this strict just for entering or leaving the capital. The security level normally applied at a higher level—the entrance to the noble district—seemed to be enforced even here.

"Yes, we are deeply concerned. Hence, moving urgently is also part of a merchant's trade, you see. We have the necessary permits and manifests. The cargo has already received inspection tags, but would you like to inspect them again here?"

Looking, Subaru saw Russel exactly at the inspection point, speaking to the guard in charge.

Beside him was "White Sheep," and Russel was presenting one document after another—carried by her in her "Perception Obstruction" robe—to the guard in rapid succession.

Under that quiet but undeniably forceful momentum, the guard seemed to be reeling.

"The documents appear to be in order, and the merchant's guild seal is indeed here. The goods in the manifest are... mainly medical supplies, I see?"

"The faster the transport, the more directly it is linked to human lives."

Leveraging his official title as a representative of the merchant's guild, and further appealing to ethics with the contents of the cargo, Russel left the guard unable to find grounds for fault-finding.

And as the finishing blow—

"Please! Without this medicine, everyone in my village will...!"

"White Sheep" grasped the guard's hand, her voice trembling, pleading with an attitude that didn't seem like an act. Pressed that far, the hesitation on the guard's face finally vanished.

With his hand held by "White Sheep," the guard cleared his throat with an air of importance.

"U-Understood. There seems to be no particular problem. Permission to pass is grant—"

"—Hold it! Who authorized skipping procedure!"

"—!"

Just as the official was about to grant permission, and "White Sheep" was about to be moved by it, a voice called halt. Reaching in from the side and butting in was an older man.

Likely the superior of the official in charge. This superior officer, having rushed out from the waiting area, snatched the documents from his subordinate's hands and, while checking the contents, said,

"A certificate from the merchant's guild, and cargo that's already been inspection-tagged. Still, rules are rules."

"A commendable view, but is this a situation where rigid adherence to rules applies?"

"A very merchant-like thing to say. But just as merchants have their ways, guards have their pride."

"I see. —Understood."

At the superior's words, the official stood at attention, and upon hearing it, Russel nodded. Even from a distance, it was clear that "White Sheep" was watching nervously, and Subaru and the others felt the same.

They knew the necessary documents were prepared, and there were dummy carriages, but—

"M-Maybe this is bad... Beako! Get into my pocket, quick!"

"You're panicking too much, I suppose! No matter how minimally pretty Betty is, I can't fit in there, I suppose! Instead, we should be more bold—"

Flustered by the possibility of the carriage being searched, Subaru and Beatrice fussed about.

Perhaps sensing the waves of agitation from Subaru and the others, Russel suddenly turned around and lightly waved his hand, as if to reassure everyone in the convoy.

Seeing that, Subaru felt relieved that there was a countermeasure— and the very next moment.

—A large tremor, a crashing sound, and then multiple screams were heard from behind the waiting line.

△▼△▼△▼△

At first, Subaru almost mistook the tremor for the dragon carriage starting, the sound for the carriage body creaking, and the voices for some kind of cheer—completely lumping them all together in a misunderstanding.

It happened so abruptly, so out of the blue.

"What the!?"

His expression changing drastically, Subaru, still holding Beatrice, jumped onto the dragon carriage's seat and looked through the large window there towards the back of the convoy—the direction from which the commotion had come.

What he saw was a disordered convoy, rising smoke, multiple figures flying out from the smoke as if in a panic, and a building visible in the same smoke, seemingly half-collapsed.

"Was that... an accident?"

"That's what it looks like, I suppose. From the looks of it, a dragon carriage with a broken axle or something caused a chain-reaction collision, I suppose."

"And the building on the roadside got crashed into, huh. This is bad, looks like there'll be a lot of injured."

As Russel said, the cause was likely the heavier-than-usual congestion due to the capital's turmoil. Precisely because it was an accident that occurred within the heavily jammed waiting line, a jostling chaos was unfolding.

He didn't know how many were injured, but they had to get people out of the accident site immediately—

"—Natsuki-dono, permission has been granted. The carriage is departing. Please take your seat."

To the panicked Subaru and Beatrice, Russel, having returned to the carriage interior, declared. Hearing those words, Subaru tried to call out "Russel-san," but—

"Eh...? What did you just say? The carriage is departing?"

"Yes. The officials have gone to deal with the accident behind us. As our documents are in order, we have been given leave to pass. There is also a need to clear the congested line of carriages."

"No, but..."

"There might be injured people, I suppose. You mentioned the cargo included medical supplies, I suppose."

"That was a disguise. The cargo does not consist solely of what's on the manifest. If we open the cargo hold, that will be discovered. I believe I told you. —Always search for the best possible outcome."

There was no sternness in his expression or voice, yet the sharpness of the content alone was exceedingly keen.

His body feeling sliced by those words, Subaru stiffened his cheeks at the chance to put it into practice so soon. —He understood Russel's point. He understood it here too, but.

"—'White Sheep,' please explain to Natsuki-dono."

"—!"

At Subaru's silence, looking down and biting his lip, Russel sighed and called out behind him. There was "White Sheep," who had just been peering into the carriage.

She choked up deep in her throat and turned her tearful, round eyes towards Russel. However, without turning towards her, Russel called her name again. "'White Sheep.'"

Hearing that, "White Sheep" decisively got into the carriage, placed her hand over Subaru's hand that was on the window frame, and—

"Subaru-san, please listen now. It's alright. I'm sure the guards will handle it properly. —It's alright."

"...'White Sheep,'-chan."

Obeying Russel, who was about to make a ruthless decision, "White Sheep" appealed to him.

For an instant, a strong rebellious feeling surged within Subaru, but the trembling of "White Sheep's" voice and hands intensely conveyed that she, too, did not truly wish to overlook this.

Desperately, with all her might, even so, she was trying to do her best. —Then, what was it that Subaru should do here?

"—Subaru."

From within his arms, Beatrice called out to him like that.

In her eyes was trust in Subaru, and the light of resolve. No matter what the assertion, she would go along with whatever Subaru chose—a light exactly the same as when she promised in the sewers to go with him even to hell.

Even if it meant shouldering sin together, Beatrice would not waver.

"For now, let's leave this..."

"Yes, indeed. A wise decision, Natsuki-dono. That is the best possible outcome for this situation."

With "White Sheep's" hand still overlapping his, Subaru's hand on the window frame was slowly lowered. Seeing Subaru's movement, Russel spoke with apparent relief.

Yes, that's right. What Russel said was correct. Here, they should follow that.

"— —"

Perhaps that accident could be called a kind of godsend.

It was just before the carriage might have been inspected and Subaru and the others discovered.

The guards' hands would be diverted to rescue efforts, and they could escape this place.

—All for the sake of saving the royal capital, the kingdom.

Therefore—

"—Ah?"

Just as he was trying to reconcile himself to the idea that pulling back his hand was the right thing to do, in the scenery outside the window that he couldn't tear his regretful eyes away from, Subaru saw it.

—The figure of "Blue Snake," the hem of their white robe fluttering, running straight towards the accident site.

"— —"

—.

— —.

— — — Are you an idiot, Natsuki Subaru?

"...The search for the best possible outcome, you told me to value that, right, Russel-san?"

"—? Yes. Your judgment is correct. Let's depart immediately. We shall..."

"Then!"

"—!?"

"Then MY best possible outcome is not abandoning anyone I can help here!!"

Raising his voice, Subaru put his hand, which he had started to lower, back onto the window frame.

And, shaking off the overlapping hand, he left a "Sorry!" to the wide-eyed "White Sheep" and Russel beyond her.

"Let's go, Beako! Lend me your strength!"

"With the utmost pleasure, leave it to me, I suppose!"

Still holding Beatrice, Subaru launched himself out of the carriage through the window. Behind him, he heard Russel calling his name, but he wouldn't stop. Couldn't stop.

The accident was a godsend? Ridiculous. An accident is a tragedy. It is absolutely not salvation.

"You were right! I was wrong!"

If he hadn't seen the back of "Blue Snake" running off, he wouldn't have even realized that.

One hundred percent, "Blue Snake" was right. Overwhelming shame surged up within him towards that retreating back.

Just how much of an idiot are you, Natsuki Subaru?

Why don't you understand, Natsuki Subaru?

Even if there are people who would willingly throw away their lives, his own standards wouldn't change.

—The fact that nothing would change at all.

"What the!? Oi, it's dangerous here! Anyone who isn't a guard—"

"There are injured people, right! I'm here to help! We'll pull everyone out!"

Right in front of the accident site, shrouded in dust, the older superior—the one who had stubbornly questioned the carriage passage earlier—widened his eyes at Subaru, who had rushed over, covering his mouth with his hand.

A man who appeared suddenly, carrying a cute little girl. Naturally, the man would be bewildered.

But that bewilderment was no reason to stop Subaru and Beatrice.

"I can use magic! We can be useful! Let us help!"

"—Tch, we can use all the hands we can get. Come on!"

A moment's hesitation—it was like a paper wall before the older guard's stance of valuing human life. Instructed by the guard who broke through it in a single breath, Subaru and Beatrice stepped into the accident site.

The space, filled with rising smoke, groans of pain, and children's crying, was, just as confirmed from afar, a chain reaction: a multiple-collision accident triggered by a dragon carriage with a broken axle, and the resulting half-collapse of a building caught in the collateral damage.

People bleeding, people lying limp and motionless. Ground dragons stomping excitedly, shouts and yells for rescue flying left and right—it was a scene of utter chaos.

Facing that scene, his gaze wandered, wondering where to start—

"—Over here! Help me over here!"

That voice was particularly loud, cutting through the cacophony of mingled voices, piercing Subaru's eardrums.

Looking, there deep within the billowing dust cloud, crouching on the ground, holding a hand over an injured person pinned under rubble, was the white robe—"Blue Snake."

Rushing over to where "Blue Snake" was, Subaru witnessed the state of the injured person—a serious wound where part of a collapsed beam had pierced their torso, causing massive blood loss.

And then—

"With Beatrice-chan's magic, lighten the beam and pull it out! You can do that, right!?"

"Yeah, I can! I can, but how do you know to—"

"Ah, jeez! Your intuition is dull!"

Speaking roughly in an exasperated tone, the next moment, "Blue Snake," who had been applying healing magic to the critically injured person's wound, roughly threw off their own hood. What appeared from underneath was flax-colored hair reaching down to the neck, and the beast-person cat ears asserting their presence as if pushing through that hair—

"—Ferris?"

Dumbfounded, Subaru's lips uttered the name.

A familiar face he hadn't expected to be there—the one who had rushed faster than anyone to a dying life, "Blue" Ferris, was desperately working.
